{
  "$comment": "Semantics every app implements (feature 016; the cases in rule-signal-cases-v1.json pin each rule). UNITS: every span and offset is in Unicode code points; spans are half-open [start, end); a signal that reads the element has spans in the element. PATTERN DIALECT: RE2 syntax on a subset, RE2 default options: case-sensitive unless ignore_case (Unicode simple case folding: k matches U+212A); '.' does not match newline; ^ and $ match only at the start and end of the text ($ never before a final newline); \\d \\w \\s \\b are ASCII and \\s is [\\t\\n\\f\\r ] (no \\v). Refused, naming the rule and the code-point position of the construct: backreference, lookaround, conditional, atomic, possessive, inline_flag, unicode_property, any_byte (\\C), repeat_too_large (a count over 1000, or nested counted repeats multiplying past 1000), too_long (over 2000 code points; position 2000), syntax (also: escapes outside \\d \\D \\s \\S \\w \\W \\b \\B \\A \\z \\a \\f \\t \\n \\r \\v \\0 \\xHH and escaped ASCII punctuation; a quantified anchor or boundary; {,n}; (?<name>; repeated group names; '[' inside a class; a class escape or reversed range as a range endpoint). NORMALISATION: text is matched as given; only declared options apply: ignore_case, and fold_fullwidth_ascii (U+FF01-U+FF5E to U+0021-U+007E, U+3000 to U+0020, one to one, applied before matching; substrings come from the original text). SIGNALS: pattern = every non-overlapping match left to right (after a match ending at e the search resumes at e, one code point later when the match was empty). proximity = for each anchor span, the nearest phrase occurrence (literal; word_boundary = ASCII \\b on both sides; each phrase matched on its own, so phrases may overlap each other) on the declared side, ignoring a phrase that overlaps the anchor; gap edge to edge (phrase end to anchor start, or anchor end to phrase start) in the declared unit (utf8_bytes = UTF-8 length of the ORIGINAL text between the two spans); ties: smaller gap, then earlier phrase in phrases, then before over after; yields the anchor span with outputs distance, phrase (as written in the text) and side when gap <= max_distance. validator = candidates from the validator's scan, from each span of an earlier signal (value = the span's text, neighbouring text on each side), or from the element; each candidate carries the validator's outputs; the signal FIRES on a candidate only when valid is true, invalid candidates stay readable by field outputs. lexical_weights = one match pass over all terms (longest term first at a position; word_boundary wraps them in ASCII \\b); score = sum of the weights of matched terms in text order, each term once under presence and once per match under occurrences; fires when score >= cut; its spans are the first match of each distinct matched term, so count is the number of distinct terms. code_rule = declared only (feature 018). A signal may refer (anchor, span_of) only to an earlier signal. EVALUATION of one row: (1) every signal's spans; (2) vetoes per candidate: each veto's 'when' expression is evaluated on the step-1 spans, then every span of each signal in 'removes' that overlaps a 'when' span (half-open: s1 < e2 and s2 < e1) is removed; vetoed is true when any span was removed; (3) expressions on the remaining spans: signal = true when it has a span, carrying its spans; all = true when every member is true, carrying every member's spans; any = true when some member is true, carrying the true members' spans; not = true when its member is false; within_n(a, b, n, unit) = true when some pair of spans of a and b has gap <= n (edge to edge; overlap is 0), carrying the contributing spans; a false expression and a not carry no spans; carried spans are sorted by start then end without duplicates; (4) outputs: fired = the expression's truth; count = number of carried spans; first = text of the first carried span, else null; spans = canonical JSON [[start, end], ...]; field = the named output of the first remaining candidate of a signal, else null. RENDERINGS, DECOYS, SUBSTITUTIONS are declared and structurally checked here; feature 017 executes renderings and decoys; substitutions are clone parameters (JSON Pointers). RENDERING SEMANTICS (every rendering has normalise(render(v)) == v cases): render builtin spaced = the value's characters joined by one space; split_lines = joined by one newline (U+000A); spelled_out_digits = each ASCII digit 0-9 written as its lower-case English word (zero one two three four five six seven eight nine), every other character kept as itself, the resulting tokens joined by one space; mapping = left to right, at each position the LONGEST key that matches there is replaced by its value, a character no key matches is kept. Normaliser builtin digits_only = keep the ASCII digits 0-9 only; strip_delimiters = remove exactly U+0020 space, U+000A newline, '-' and '.'; words_to_digits = the inverse of spelled_out_digits: read the text as tokens separated by exactly one space, a token that is a digit word becomes its digit and any other token is one kept character; mapping_inverse = the mapping inverted (its values must be distinct), applied the same way (longest key first). IDENTITY: sha256 of the RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical JSON of the document as received (the parsed JSON values before any default; never a re-dump of a model): keys sorted by UTF-16 code units, no whitespace, numbers as values in ECMAScript form (1 and 1.0 are the same number, 1e21 is 1e+21), strings UTF-8 with only the quote, backslash and U+0000-U+001F escaped. An ABSENT list and an EMPTY list differ: an omitted vetoes and vetoes: [] are different documents with different digests. An integer outside +/-(2^53 - 1) is refused (unsafe_integer, naming its JSON Pointer). Strings must be NFC. BOUNDS (settings, refused naming the setting, never truncated): RULE_SET_MAX_SIGNALS, RULE_SET_MAX_CASES, RULE_SET_MAX_DOCUMENT_BYTES, RULE_SET_MAX_TERMS (lexical terms, default 2000), RULE_SET_MAX_CODE_PARAMS_BYTES (code_rule params, default 16 KiB).",
